## First refusals and isolated corrections

The first native attempt passes all four baseline commands and candidate check on
1.94. Candidate strict Clippy then refuses two fixture defects:

- `duplicate_mod`: both majors' kernel modules load the same physical ReceiverPin
  path, despite its parent channel type differing.
- `type_complexity`: the sandbox entrypoint's compile-only function-pointer
  signature expands its tuple result inline.

The complete first implementation, frozen inputs and streams remain. There is no
lint allowance. A separate private native root changes only the second receiver
module filename, copies the original ReceiverPin bytes unchanged to that filename,
and simplifies the anchor's signature. The kernel body is otherwise exact. Both
isolated locks remain byte-identical to the first attempt. Only vendor-directory
locations change in each copied Cargo config. Four already passing unchanged
baseline commands are reused; the corrected candidate passes check/strict Clippy
on both compilers.

The first offline controls also retain one failed assertion and one error. The
assertion incorrectly treated the resolved workspace lock as an unchanged test
asset. The error shows that a rejected action reached the parent command's runtime
receipt read before the local action whitelist. The corrected control distinguishes
the intentional lock write; the command rejects invalid phase labels before any
parent receipt read. Nine corrected controls pass. These are fixture controls,
not a new production fault matrix or a retry of an unchanged failing candidate.

